<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
<title>holdfast</title>
<style>
/*holdfast:inline-css*/
</style>
</head>
<body>
<header>
  <h1>hold<span class="mono">fast</span></h1>
  <span class="grow"></span>
  <span id="conn">connecting…</span>
</header>
<!-- Polite, atomic screen-reader summary. The SSE stream pushes a fresh snapshot on
     every job transition; announcing the whole table would spam a screen-reader reader
     on each one, so we announce only a short count summary, and only when it changes. -->
<div id="sr-status" class="visually-hidden" role="status" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true"></div>
<main>
  <!-- The page is ordered by the two questions an operator actually has, in the order
       they ask them: what is holdfast doing right now, and what has it done to my
       library. Everything about the CURRENT RUN comes first and everything derived from
       the ledger comes after, each under its own heading, so a reader working down the
       page (or a screen reader working through the headings) answers the live question
       before meeting a single whole-library figure. -->
  <section id="region-now" aria-labelledby="h-now">
    <h2 id="h-now">Right now</h2>
    <p class="note">The live state of this holdfast: whether it is running or paused, whether a scan is
      under way, how many files stand in each state, and the work in flight. Every figure in this region
      describes THIS run and is pushed by the server as it changes.</p>
    <div class="badges">
      <span id="b-paused" class="badge">running</span>
      <span id="b-scan" class="badge scan">idle</span>
      <span class="reclaimed">reclaimed this run: <b id="reclaimed-session">0 B</b></span>
    </div>

    <div class="chips" id="chips"></div>

    <div class="controls">
      <label for="token" class="lbl">Control token</label>
      <input id="token" type="password" placeholder="for pause / rescan" autocomplete="off">
      <button id="rescan" class="primary">Rescan</button>
      <button id="pause">Pause</button>
      <button id="resume">Resume</button>
      <span id="msg" role="status" aria-live="polite"></span>
    </div>

    <div class="controls">
      <label for="filter" class="lbl">Filter</label>
      <input id="filter" type="search" placeholder="filter queue &amp; history by path…" autocomplete="off">
    </div>

    <h3>Queue &amp; active</h3>
    <p class="note">Elapsed is how long the file has been in the state it is in now, recomputed from the
      transition timestamp on each update rather than counted in this page. Progress is read from the
      encoder's own progress stream and measured against the source duration, so it is shown while a file
      is <i>encoding</i> and not in any other state — a file being probed or verified is covered by
      Elapsed alone. Where the encoder has reported nothing yet, or the container reports no duration, it
      reads <i>unknown</i> — never an estimate, and never a stale figure.</p>
    <p class="note cap" id="queue-cap" hidden></p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table><thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Status</th><th>Elapsed</th><th>Progress</th><th>Worker</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody id="queue"></tbody></table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="region-history" aria-labelledby="h-history">
    <h2 id="h-history">What it has done to your library</h2>
    <p class="note">Everything in this region is derived from the ledger rather than from the run above,
      so it survives restarts and describes the library as a whole.
      <span class="reclaimed">reclaimed lifetime: <b id="reclaimed-lifetime">0 B</b></span></p>

    <h3>Across the whole ledger</h3>
    <p class="note">Every figure here is computed by the server over <em>every</em> matching row in the
      ledger, not over the capped rows the tables below ship, and each states the set it covers. A row
      that recorded no value is excluded and counted, never read as a zero: a fact nobody measured is
      reported as missing, not as 0. VMAF figures pool per-file scores, each measured against that
      file's own source under one viewing condition, luma-only; they are not compared between files.</p>
    <p class="note">Each figure is also DRAWN, from the same server-computed numbers and nothing else:
      the bars of a distribution are in proportion to the counts beside them, and a spread puts its
      minimum, mean and maximum on one scale. The drawing carries no value the text beside it does not
      carry, so a reader using a screen reader, a keyboard or no colour vision loses nothing by ignoring
      it, and no figure is readable only by pointing at it.</p>
    <div class="aggs" id="aggregates"></div>

    <h3>Recent history</h3>
    <p class="note">Each finished file, with the proof its swap was safe. A skipped file shows which
      guard held it back; a failed one shows why. VMAF is a perceptual estimate under one viewing
      condition — the model, its pooling, and its luma-only blind spot are shown with every score, and
      scores are never compared across files (VMAF is not comparable between different sources).</p>
    <p class="note cap" id="hist-cap" hidden></p>
    <div class="tablewrap">
      <table class="hist">
        <colgroup>
          <col style="width:19%"><col style="width:17%"><col style="width:12%"><col style="width:23%">
          <col style="width:8%"><col style="width:9%"><col style="width:12%">
        </colgroup>
        <thead><tr>
          <th>File</th><th>Result</th><th>Size</th><th>VMAF</th><th>Encoder</th><th>Encode</th><th>Updated</th>
        </tr></thead>
        <tbody id="history"></tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>
</main>
<footer>holdfast — config-as-code, data-safe media transcoder. The YAML config is the source of truth;
  this page reads and controls. It reports what was measured, and only that.
  <!--holdfast:source-offer--></footer>

<!-- Row shells cloned per render. Rows are built as DOM nodes (createElement +
     textContent) and filled from cloned templates — the page never assembles an HTML
     string from job data, so an attacker-influencable media path is inert text, never
     markup. Trusted Types (enforced by the response CSP) makes that a hard guarantee. -->
<template id="tpl-queue-row">
  <tr>
    <td class="path"></td>
    <td class="st"><span class="dot"></span><span class="stlabel"></span></td>
    <td class="elapsed"></td>
    <td class="prog"></td>
    <td class="worker"></td>
  </tr>
</template>
<template id="tpl-agg">
  <div class="agg">
    <h3 class="agg-k"></h3>
    <div class="agg-v"></div>
    <p class="agg-cov"></p>
    <p class="agg-ex"></p>
  </div>
</template>
<template id="tpl-hist-row">
  <tr>
    <td class="path"></td>
    <td class="st"></td>
    <td class="size"></td>
    <td class="vmaf"></td>
    <td class="enc"></td>
    <td class="dur"></td>
    <td class="upd"></td>
  </tr>
</template>
<!-- The figure shells. Every graphic on this page is an inline vector drawing whose SHAPE
     is markup written here and whose only variable is a geometry attribute the renderer
     sets from a number the server published. Cloning them is what keeps the graphics
     inside the same idiom as the rows: no library, no image, no font, no data: URI, and
     no element built from a string. The SVG namespace comes from the HTML parser reading
     these templates, so no module needs to name it either.

     Each drawing is aria-hidden and not focusable, because its values are carried in full
     by the text the renderer puts beside it - announcing both would read every number
     twice. -->
<template id="tpl-fig-bar">
  <svg class="fig bar" width="100%" height="9" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" preserveAspectRatio="none">
    <rect class="mark" x="0" y="0" width="0" height="9"></rect>
  </svg>
</template>
<template id="tpl-fig-spread">
  <svg class="fig spread" viewBox="0 0 1000 22" width="100%" height="22" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false" preserveAspectRatio="none">
    <line class="axis" x1="2" y1="11" x2="998" y2="11" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></line>
    <line class="tick min" x1="2" y1="4" x2="2" y2="18" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></line>
    <line class="tick mean" x1="500" y1="0" x2="500" y2="22" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></line>
    <line class="tick max" x1="998" y1="4" x2="998" y2="18" vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke"></line>
  </svg>
</template>

<script>
//holdfast:inline-js
</script>
</body>
</html>
